        <h2>Chemotherapy: What to expect</h2>
        <p>Chemotherapy is a systemic cancer treatment. Chemotherapy drugs travel through your bloodstream to treat cancer throughout the body.</p>

        <h3>Ways you might receive chemotherapy</h3>
        <p>You might receive chemotherapy in several ways, depending on the medicines used and your treatment plan.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Infusion:</strong> A nurse places an IV into a vein in your hand or arm so medicine can enter your bloodstream.</li>
          <li><strong>Port:</strong> A radiologist or surgeon places a small device under your skin so your care team can deliver medication without repeated needle sticks.</li>
          <li><strong>Central line:</strong> A surgeon places a long, thin tube under the skin that a nurse can use to deliver medication.</li>
          <li><strong>Pills:</strong> Some chemotherapy medications come in pills or capsules you swallow, called oral chemotherapy.</li>
          <li><strong>Under the skin:</strong> A member of your care team injects medicine right under your skin. This is called subcutaneous delivery.</li>
          <li><strong>Breathing in:</strong> Some chemotherapy drugs can be inhaled to deliver medicine directly to an affected area, such as the lungs.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>About our infusion center</h3>
        <p>You can receive chemotherapy services in the infusion center at Mays Cancer Center. Our infusion center is set up with comfortable chairs, televisions, Wi-Fi and ample space for you to bring a family member or friend along for support.</p>
        <p>Our doctors and oncology-trained nurses have decades of experience working with chemotherapy drugs. They use this knowledge to help you avoid or manage side effects, such as nausea.</p>

        <h2>How to talk to your doctor about chemotherapy</h2>
        <p>Your care team will consider many factors, such as the type of cancer and your overall health, when deciding which chemotherapy drugs are right for you. You might receive one or more chemotherapy drugs at one time.</p>
        <p>Before you start chemotherapy, our team will sit down with you to explain what your treatment entails and what you can expect, including possible side effects.</p>
        <p>We encourage you to ask questions at any time. Bringing a family member or friend along to your appointments can help you remember treatment details or key points you wanted to address.</p>

        {# Optional FAQ accordion - use when a treatment page has useful questions not already answered in the main content. #}
